  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Ledger at Dusk",
    "overview": "Money Hub is a bookkeeper's ledger read under a lamp. Warm paper in daylight, warm near-black at night, and figures that sit still on the page. The interface does no persuading: it lays out what the database computed, in a fixed order, so the eye goes to the number and then to what's due next.\n\nComponents are crisp and utilitarian. Hairline rules separate things, one deep pine accent marks the single thing you can act on, and status color (in, caution, overdue) is the only other color allowed to speak. Density is moderate: phone-first stacks of cards, small labels over large tabular figures, and 40px minimum touch targets. Dusk (dark mode) is a peer of daylight, not an inversion: every token has a tuned dark value.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Warm neutral paper and ink in both themes, never pure white-on-black or blue-gray.",
      "One accent, Deep Pine, for primary actions, the active nav item and focus rings.",
      "Tabular figures everywhere money appears.",
      "Flat surfaces; a near-invisible shadow on cards, real shadow only on things that float.",
      "System font stack; hierarchy comes from size and weight, not typeface."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Neutral Spend Rule",
        "body": "Money out is never red. Red means overdue or an error, not \"you spent money\". Only money in gets color.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Pine Rule",
        "body": "Deep Pine marks what you can act on or where you are. It is never a background for content or a decorative stripe.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Dusk Parity Rule",
        "body": "No color ships without its dark value. Add new colors as CSS custom properties with both steps, never as raw hex in a component.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Rule",
        "body": "Every money value renders with tabular figures, so columns of amounts align digit for digit.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The 16px Input Rule",
        "body": "Inputs are 1rem on phones (0.875rem from `sm` up) so iOS Safari never zooms on focus.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Only-Floaters-Cast Rule",
        "body": "Only elements that float over content (the + button, the sheet) get a visible shadow. Nothing in the page flow does.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** take every color from a CSS custom property with light and dark values (`web/src/index.css`).",
      "**Do** render money through the shared `Money` / `Stat` / `Row` components so tabular figures and sign color stay consistent.",
      "**Do** keep touch targets at least 40px (44px for phone inputs, 56px for tabs and the + button).",
      "**Do** use the status tones only for status: ok, caution, overdue.",
      "**Do** reuse `web/src/components/ui.tsx` primitives before adding new ones."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** color money out red or orange; red is for overdue and errors.",
      "**Don't** add a second accent color or use Deep Pine as a decorative fill.",
      "**Don't** add shadows to in-flow elements beyond the card's sheet-rest shadow.",
      "**Don't** introduce a webfont or a separate numeric font; the system stack with tabular figures is the type system.",
      "**Don't** use chart series colors outside charts."
    ]
  }
}